## What this fork changes

- Package identity: `dsh-chrome-suite`, plugin row `chrome-suite`, settings route
  `/dsh-chrome-suite/panel`, locale namespace `dsh-chrome-suite`.
- Adds a `browser_status` diagnostic tool that reports host/extension state without reading pages.
- Adds a configurable action allowlist (`allowedActions`, also reads
  `DSH_CHROME_USE_ALLOWED_ACTIONS`) enforced before every tool executes.
- Replaces the settings card's dynamic import of DSH client UI primitives with self-contained
  controls, and marks a stale status when polling fails instead of showing an old "connected".
- Renames the bundled Chrome extension's display name to "Chrome Suite Bridge" (version 1.15.1) so it
  is distinguishable from the upstream store copy, and adds an in-panel privacy notice.
- Gives the fork its own transport identity: host name `com.chromesuite.bridge`, install locations
  `~/.dsh/chrome-suite-bridge` and `~/chrome-suite-bridge-extension`, socket/token/log files under
  `/tmp/chrome-suite-bridge-<uid>/`, and a manifest `key` generated for this fork. The upstream store
  extension ID is no longer allowed to reach this host.
- Removes the upstream "nudge the legacy host" path, which would have sent a reload to the upstream
  host on a machine where both are installed.
- Host names must match Chrome's grammar `[a-z0-9_]+(\.[a-z0-9_]+)*`: hyphens are rejected with
  "Invalid native messaging host name specified", so this fork uses `com.chromesuite.bridge`.
- Consolidates the Chinese and English documentation and adds a comparison with `dsh-browser-cdp`.

## What this fork deliberately does not change

- The CDP operation set, the native-messaging wire protocol, and the in-page cursor behaviour.

Because the transport identity is separate, installing this fork does not overwrite the upstream
bridge, and the two extension IDs are distinct. Running both plugin rows at the same time is still
not recommended: each attaches a debugger to the same Chrome tabs and registers overlapping
`browser_*` tools.

